CORRECTS the arithmetic estimate in value_freq_skip_ab_2026-09-10.meta, which
composed two ratios to predict "e3 at 1024^2 t=1 about 1.19x" and explicitly
flagged that as not measured. Measured, it is **1.25x** — the composition was
optimistic, because the two fixes overlap in what they speed up.

WHAT MOVED, AND WHY IT IS WHERE IT IS:
  - **e3 at >= 1 MP was the worst band on the ladder and is no longer the worst
    anywhere on 8 threads**: 2048 t=8 goes 1.19 -> **0.94**, i.e. from 19 %
    slower than cjxl to 6 % faster. t=1 remains above parity (1.23-1.25).
  - **e5 thread scaling improved but is still the weakest axis**: 1024 t=8
    1.32 -> 1.14 and 2048 t=8 1.47 -> **1.32**, which is now the single loosest
    cell in the grid.
  - **64x64 is unchanged, as expected.** At that size the encode is 4-5 ms and
    dominated by per-call fixed cost, so parallelising per-pixel phases has
    nothing to bite on. Do not read the flat 64^2 row as a failed fix.
  - e9 t=1 is unchanged at 1.03-1.05 (perceptual-loop bound); e9 t=8 stays
    0.44-0.46.

STILL OPEN, in priority order: e5 at threads=8 (1.32 at 2048), e3 at threads=1
(1.23-1.25), and the byte intercept at 64x64 where we are still 9-22 % larger
than cjxl (unchanged — nothing here touched bytes).
